1935: \begin{proposition}[Minimal bad-record witness for command-run escalation]
1936: \label{prop:publication-safety-minimal-bad-record-witness}
1937: In the operational interface of
1938: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}, let $r$ be a submitted
1939: claim record whose claim-kind set is \{route\}, whose evidence level is declared
1940: to be command-run, and whose boundary field contains no command-run tuple
1941: $(\text{command},\text{source state},\text{environment},\text{exit code},
1942: \text{log path})$.  Suppose all other route coordinates of $r$ are filled:
1943: there is an evidence pointer, a human-promotion marker at the declared boundary,
1944: and pass rows for the promotion, overlap, and submission-state gates.  Then the
1945: schema verifier rejects $r$, and the inclusion-minimal bad subrecord is exactly
1946: the pair consisting of the evidence-level declaration ``command-run'' and the
1947: boundary field missing the five command-run coordinates.  In particular, adding
1948: more prose, advisory scores, route history, or path-verified evidence surfaces
1949: cannot repair the rejected stronger reading unless the missing command-run tuple
1950: is added as a finite coordinate.
1951: \end{proposition}
1952: 
1953: \begin{proof}
1954: For a route claim, the dispatch table in
1955: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} requires the promotion,
1956: overlap, and submission-state gates.  By hypothesis those pass rows and the
1957: route-boundary marker are already present, so clauses A1--A4 and A6 of
1958: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} have no missing route,
1959: evidence-surface, gate-coverage, or promotion coordinate to report.  The only
1960: remaining clause that reads the declared evidence level is the command-run part
1961: of the boundary condition: at command-run strength the boundary field must
1962: contain the command, immutable source state, environment, exit code, and log
1963: path.  These five fields are absent by hypothesis, so the verifier rejects.
1964: 
1965: The bad subrecord is inclusion-minimal.  If the evidence-level declaration is
1966: removed, the command-run boundary obligation is no longer triggered by the
1967: record.  If the boundary field is replaced by one containing the five command-run
1968: coordinates, the displayed obstruction disappears and the record must be tested
1969: against the remaining clauses, which were assumed filled.  Deleting any unrelated
1970: prose, advisory score, route-history note, or path-verified surface does not add
1971: the missing five fields and therefore cannot change the failed command-run
1972: clause.  Thus the rejected stronger reading is witnessed by a finite bad record,
1973: not by a semantic judgment about the surrounding pipeline.  This is the concrete
1974: negative instance used by Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface},
1975: Proposition~\ref{prop:publication-safety-maximality}, and
1976: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} when a path-verified route
1977: or submission row is silently upgraded to command-run strength.
1978: \end{proof}
1979: \begin{theorem}[Finite-record safety interface under record-gate soundness]
1980: \label{thm:publication-safety-interface}
1981: Consider a theory-to-paper pipeline with the operational interface above and a
1982: finite submitted record surface consisting only of the manuscript source, printed
1983: tables, submitted manifests, uploaded review-bundle records, recorded logs, and
1984: explicitly declared historical boundaries.  Assume the following four distinct
1985: finite-record hypotheses.
1986: \begin{enumerate}
1987: \item \emph{Acceptance entries}: every load-bearing claim in an active manuscript
1988: is either rejected or has an acceptance entry with an evidence surface inside
1989: this finite submitted record surface.
1990: \item \emph{Record-gate soundness}: writing $S$ for this finite submitted record
1991: surface and $L$ for the acceptance ledger, the package satisfies the finite
1992: predicate $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ of
1993: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} as delimited by
1994: Theorem~\ref{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}: every recorded
1995: acceptance entry has the assigned passing rows and submitted evidence pointers,
1996: every advisory output is demoted unless the deterministic gate set $\Gamma$ for
1997: its claim kind passes, and no independent implementation-soundness,
1998: fresh-execution, or semantic-correctness claim about the gates is inferred unless
1999: the package also contains the corresponding source path, command, source state,
2000: environment, exit code, and log path.
2001: \item \emph{Discovery certificate input}: discovery claims are checked through
2002: the pinned discovery-gate interface certified by
2003: Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} and
2004: Propositions~\ref{prop:reviewable-source-snapshot-interface},
2005: \ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency}, and
2006: \ref{prop:source-interface-evidence-level-separation}, in particular the
2007: separated manual/path-verified and executable finite-record levels for the
2008: pipeline safety and main pipeline theorem labels.
2009: \item \emph{Promotion and verifier boundary}: no inactive intake seed is treated
2010: as publication work without human promotion, and the constructed tuple below is
2011: submitted to the finite schema verifier rather than being identified with the
2012: acceptance entry itself.
2013: \end{enumerate}
2014: Then, and only under these finite-record hypotheses, the package checker has the
2015: following finite-record invariant.  The checker input is the submitted surface
2016: $S$, the ledger $L$, the dispatch table $\Gamma$, and the schema files and bundle
2017: records named in Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}.  Its
2018: output is the finite accepted set
2019: \[
2020: \mathsf{Out}_{\Gamma,S}(L)=
2021: \{(c,\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c)):\ V(c,\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c))=1\}
2022: \]
2023: together with the finite list of rejected bad subrecords.  For every accepted
2024: load-bearing claim $c$ it constructs, from the acceptance entry and the submitted
2025: finite evidence surfaces, a record
2026: \[
2027: \mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c)=\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)
2028: \]
2029: accepted by the finite schema verifier of
2030: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} with the input--predicate--output
2031: boundary of Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}; equivalently, by the
2032: finite-record exactness of Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, the
2033: constructed record satisfies the schema-relative record-acceptance semantics for
2034: the dispatch table $\Gamma$.  Consequently, within the submitted finite records
2035: and under the stated explicit record-gate-soundness hypothesis, $c$ has a recorded evidence
2036: surface, a declared evidence boundary, and a prior pass record for every gate
2037: required for its claim kind.  Accepted discovery claims additionally inherit the
2038: bounded pinned source-interface consequences that statement-only, code-only,
2039: mechanical-expansion, and compression-only outputs are not accepted as discovery
2040: without the required certificate, ledger, and lineage witnesses.  Moreover, the
2041: constructed record is axis-and-level invariant and sharp for the stronger-reading
2042: preorder: if $c\preceq_{\Gamma}c^{+}$ is a properly stronger reading that moves
2043: a load-bearing consequence from one architecture axis of
2044: Definition~\ref{def:architecture-axis-map} to another, raises the evidence level
2045: from path-verified to command-run strength, adds an implementation, artifact,
2046: venue, or semantic-correctness assertion, or adds a theorem-obligation
2047: completeness assertion, then $c^{+}$ is accepted by the same interface if and only
2048: if the submitted record surface contains the corresponding finite extension
2049: coordinates and those records pass the assigned gates.  If those coordinates are
2050: absent, the accepted certificate is maximal below $c^{+}$: it still accepts $c$
2051: and rejects the stronger reading by a finite bad subrecord.
2052: 
2053: The maximality of this constructed certificate is stated separately in
2054: Proposition~\ref{prop:publication-safety-maximality}, and the exact finite price
2055: of any genuine strengthening is classified by
2056: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}; the current-package
2057: instantiation is stated separately in
2058: Corollary~\ref{cor:publication-safety-current-package-instance} and refined by
2059: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}.
2060: \end{theorem}
2061: 
2062: \begin{proof}
2063: We verify the obligations in the order advertised in the statement.
2064: 
2065: \emph{Package-checker construction.}  The checker first reads the finite record
2066: surface $S$ and ledger $L$; no prose paragraph or live daemon state is queried.
2067: For each ledger row it forms the candidate tuple
2068: $(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$, runs the six predicates $P_1,\ldots,P_6$, appends
2069: $(c,\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c))$ to $\mathsf{Out}_{\Gamma,S}(L)$ on acceptance,
2070: and otherwise appends the least failed predicate together with the fields read by
2071: that predicate to the rejected-subrecord list.  Let $c$ be an accepted
2072: load-bearing claim in the manuscript.  The acceptance-entry hypothesis supplies
2073: the claim-kind set $K$, evidence level $\ell$, and evidence surface $E$, so the
2074: decision is attached to a source path, artifact path, command log, inventory, or
2075: witness record rather than to generated prose alone.  Record-gate-soundness
2076: supplies the pass-record field $R$ for every gate assigned by $\Gamma$.  If
2077: accepting $c$ crosses from intake or source material into daemon-visible
2078: publication work, the promotion assumption supplies the human-promotion field
2079: $H$; otherwise $H$ records that no such boundary is crossed.  The evidence-level
2080: definition supplies the boundary field $B$: either the command-run fields are
2081: present or the record explicitly remains path-verified.  If $c$ is a discovery
2082: claim, the discovery-routing assumption supplies $D$ from the pinned
2083: source-interface rows; otherwise $D$ is absent as required by the certificate
2084: schema.  Thus the constructor
2085: \(\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c)=\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\) is determined by
2086: finite submitted records and is locally checkable whenever its cited paths and
2087: manifest rows lie in the submitted artifact, review bundle, or explicit
2088: historical boundary.  The output invariant follows from the same case split and
2089: from Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}: accepted rows are exactly
2090: those for which all six predicates hold, and rejected rows carry the finite bad
2091: subrecord defined by the first failed predicate, with no output channel for
2092: end-to-end semantic correctness, fresh case reruns, or venue acceptance.  The
2093: same construction verifies $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}(c,\mathcal C)$, so
2094: \emph{Record exactness.}  The finite-record exactness of
2095: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} identifies the constructed
2096: certificate with the paper's independently stated record-acceptance semantics,
2097: not merely with an informal unpacking of the tuple.
2098: 
2099: \emph{Axis and evidence-level invariant.}  The record just constructed exposes
2100: exactly the axes and levels on which $c$ is allowed to rely.  If a proposed
2101: consequence of $c$ needs evidence on a different architecture axis,
2102: Proposition~\ref{prop:architecture-axis-non-escalation} forces a separate
2103: submitted coordinate for that axis and a pass record for the gates assigned to
2104: that coordinate.  If the proposed consequence instead raises the evidence level
2105: from path-verified to command-run strength, clause~A6 of
2106: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} forces the command,
2107: source state, environment, exit code, and log path into $B$.  In both cases,
2108: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} identifies the added
2109: finite coordinates as the positive extension budget for the stronger reading.
2110: If those coordinates are absent, Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}
2111: identifies the proposed assertion as a properly stronger reading without the
2112: extension coordinates required for acceptance.  Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}
2113: then returns the same original certificate and no cross-axis, cross-level,
2114: implementation, artifact-semantic, venue, or theorem-completeness consequence is
2115: certified.  The failed coordinate is a finite bad subrecord because it is either a
2116: missing kind in $K$, a missing gate record in $R$, a missing command tuple in $B$,
2117: or an evidence item outside the submitted surface $E$.
2118: 
2119: \emph{Gate coverage.}  Propositions~\ref{prop:claim-kind-dispatch} and
2120: \ref{prop:gate-interface-separation} identify the required finite gate set:
2121: formal-source claims use build and axiom gates, traceability claims use inventory
2122: and marker gates, route claims use promotion, overlap, and submission-state
2123: gates, artifact claims use status and limitation-ledger gates, submission claims
2124: use submission-pack gates, extension-style discovery claims use the
2125: theorem-content delta gate together with the pinned discovery gate,
2126: work-selection claims use the scheduler gate, and mixed claims use the union.
2127: Thus $c$ has a prior pass record for the gates required by its kind.  The
2128: advisory-output assumption prevents an agent draft, review packet, score, or
2129: repair proposal from being promoted by prose alone: without the relevant pass
2130: records it remains demoted.  The promotion assumption similarly prevents an
2131: inactive intake seed from becoming publication work merely because a local source
2132: path exists.
2133: 
2134: \emph{Discovery boundary.}  If $c$ is a discovery claim, the discovery-routing assumption sends it through
2135: the pinned discovery-gate interface.  Proposition~\ref{prop:reviewable-source-snapshot-interface}
2136: supplies the corresponding reviewer-facing snapshot interface, so this routing
2137: does not depend on a reviewer's access to the author's absolute source path.  The
2138: record-file consistency certificate in
2139: Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency} further shows, at
2140: the finite record-comparison level, that the manuscript table, JSON mirror,
2141: review-bundle manifest, digest, line count, and bundled source snapshot name the
2142: same finite imported interface.  The
2143: source-interface table records the imported statements used here:
2144: statement-only, code-only, mechanical-expansion, and compression-only records do
2145: not become accepted discovery claims without the required certificate, ledger,
2146: and lineage witnesses, and classifier-shift selection remains bounded by the
2147: discovery gate as shown in Proposition~\ref{prop:scheduler-selection-advisory}.
2148: Finally, the constructed tuple is accepted by
2149: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}; equivalently,
2150: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} identifies it with
2151: $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}$.  The stronger non-escalation and maximality statement is
2152: Proposition~\ref{prop:publication-safety-maximality}; the finite extension-budget
2153: normal form in Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} identifies
2154: exactly which additional finite coordinates a stronger reading would have to add.
2155: The current theorem is therefore the finite certificate-construction component of
2156: the safety interface, with a separately proved budget boundary for extensions.
2157: No current-package conclusion is used in this proof; the package instantiation
2158: appears only later, after its support-surface hypotheses have been stated.
2159: \end{proof}
2160: 
2161: For reviewer reproducibility, the constructor and extractor used in
2162: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} can be written as the following
2163: finite algorithms.  The constructor \(\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}\) reads, in order,
2164: the claim-kind coordinates from the acceptance entry, the declared evidence level,
2165: the cited submitted surfaces, the pass rows assigned by \(\Gamma\), the route
2166: promotion field, the pinned discovery rows when the kind set contains discovery,
2167: and the command-boundary field.  The bad-record extractor \(\mathsf{Bad}_{\Gamma}\)
2168: applies the six schema predicates in the order of
2169: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract} and returns the fields read by
2170: the first false predicate.  Thus positive certificates and negative certificates
2171: are built from the same finite coordinates; neither construction consults live
2172: daemon state, proof-assistant semantics, artifact semantics, or venue state.
2173: 
2174: \begin{proposition}[Certificate constructor and bad-subrecord extractor]
2175: \label{prop:certificate-constructor-bad-subrecord-extractor}
2176: Assume the four finite-record hypotheses of
2177: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.  For every ledger row \(c\) there
2178: is a deterministic partial constructor
2179: \[
2180: \mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)
2181: \]
2182: and a deterministic extractor \(\mathsf{Bad}_{\Gamma}(c)\) with the following
2183: properties.
2184: \begin{enumerate}
2185: \item \(\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c)\) is defined and accepted if and only if the
2186: six-clause predicate \(\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}(c,\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c))\) holds.
2187: \item If \(\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c)\) is rejected, then
2188: \(\mathsf{Bad}_{\Gamma}(c)\) is an inclusion-minimal finite bad subrecord of one
2189: of the five obstruction skeletons in
2190: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}: missing submitted
2191: evidence, missing assigned gate record, missing route or external decision field,
2192: missing pinned discovery row, or unsupported command-run boundary.
2193: \item For every properly stronger reading \(c^{+}\) of an accepted row, the same
2194: extractor rejects \(c^{+}\) exactly at the first unpaid positive extension
2195: coordinate of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}; if no such
2196: coordinate is added, \(c^{+}\) is not distinct from \(c\) in the finite-record
2197: preorder.
2198: \end{enumerate}
2199: Consequently the publication-safety theorem has both a positive certificate
2200: construction algorithm and a negative bad-subrecord extraction algorithm, each
2201: bounded by the submitted schema fields.
2202: \end{proposition}
2203: 
2204: \begin{proof}
2205: The constructor is obtained by the ``Package-checker construction'' paragraph of
2206: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}: from the acceptance entry it reads
2207: \(K\), \(\ell\), and \(E\); from record-gate soundness it reads the gate-pass rows
2208: \(R\) assigned by \(\Gamma\); from the promotion hypothesis it reads \(H\) or the
2209: no-crossing marker; from discovery routing it reads \(D\) exactly for discovery
2210: claims; and from the evidence-level definition it reads \(B\), either the
2211: path-verified boundary or the five command-run fields.  These are finite fields
2212: on the submitted surface, so the constructor is deterministic and partial only
2213: when a required field is absent.  By
2214: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, acceptance of the constructed tuple
2215: is equivalent to \(\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}\), proving the first item.
2216: 
2217: For rejection, run the predicates \(P_1,\ldots,P_6\) of
2218: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract} in order and return the finite
2219: fields read by the first false predicate.  If the failure is not merely an
2220: out-of-domain kind or level, it is one of the five substantive coordinates
2221: \(E,R,H,D,B\).  The exhaustion argument of
2222: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} proves that these are
2223: exactly the five listed obstruction skeletons, and deleting unread coordinates
2224: gives inclusion-minimality.  If the failure is an out-of-domain kind or level,
2225: the same theorem reduces it to the corresponding assigned-gate or evidence-level
2226: boundary obstruction, since \(K\) determines the finite gate set and \(\ell\)
2227: determines the boundary obligation.
2228: 
2229: Finally let \(c^{+}\) be a proper stronger reading of an accepted row.  By
2230: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, such a reading changes at
2231: least one finite coordinate unless it is record-equivalent to \(c\).  The first
2232: changed coordinate not supplied on the submitted support surface is read by one
2233: of the six predicates and is therefore returned by the extractor.  If all changed
2234: coordinates are supplied and pass their assigned gates, the enlarged tuple is a
2235: new accepted certificate for the enlarged claim; if none are changed, the finite
2236: preorder has not distinguished \(c^{+}\) from \(c\).\end{proof}
2237: 
2238: \begin{proposition}[Finite-record maximality]
2239: \label{prop:publication-safety-maximality}
2240: Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, the
2241: constructed certificate is maximal with respect to the finite submitted record
2242: surface.  More precisely, let $c$ be an accepted load-bearing claim and let
2243: $\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ be the certificate constructed in
2244: that theorem.  If a stronger reading $c^{+}$ asserts any external end-to-end
2245: compiler correctness, current execution of unrecorded gates, proof-assistant
2246: correctness, current formal-source currency, dynamic artifact semantics, or venue
2247: acceptance, then either $c^{+}$ is not a distinct finite-record consequence of
2248: $\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c)$, or its certificate must strictly enlarge one of
2249: $K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B$ by adding the corresponding claim kind, evidence surface,
2250: gate pass record, promotion or venue field, discovery-interface row, or
2251: command-run tuple.  In the latter case, if the enlargement is absent, a finite
2252: bad subrecord is returned by Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} or by
2253: one of the obstruction types of
2254: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}.
2255: \end{proposition}
2256: 
2257: \begin{proof}
2258: The schema verifier used in Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} reads
2259: only the tuple \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\), the dispatch table $\Gamma$, and the cited
2260: submitted finite surfaces.  This is exactly the finite-coordinate determination
2261: boundary of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.  Hence two
2262: surrounding implementations, formal-source trees, artifact runs, or venue states
2263: that agree on those coordinates obtain the same verifier decision.  A purported
2264: stronger reading which changes none of the coordinates is therefore invisible to
2265: the certificate and cannot become a new accepted finite-record conclusion.
2266: 
2267: If the stronger reading is made load-bearing, it changes a coordinate.  External
2268: compiler, proof-assistant, formal-source-currency, or artifact-semantics claims
2269: add claim kinds and assigned gate records; fresh executions raise the evidence
2270: boundary to command-run data; route or venue assertions add promotion or venue
2271: surfaces; discovery-strengthening adds source-interface rows.  These are exactly
2272: the finite fields tested by the clauses of
2273: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}.  When the required
2274: field is missing, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} supplies the
2275: failed acceptance clause, and
2276: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} classifies the failure
2277: as a missing evidence surface, gate record, promotion field, discovery-interface
2278: row, or command-run escalation.\end{proof}
2279: 
2280: \begin{corollary}[Current-package source-level instance of the safety interface]
2281: \label{cor:publication-safety-current-package-instance}
2282: For the current package, assume the support-surface condition later closed by
2283: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure}: the exact supplemental
2284: \path{review_bundle/} directory is uploaded with the submission, or an
2285: archive-equivalent replacement with matching final digest records is supplied.
2286: Under that explicit availability condition, the finite pass-row components of
2287: the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} are witnessed
2288: by the machine-readable evidence map of
2289: Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}, the acceptance
2290: ledger of
2291: Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger}, the
2292: assigned-gate boundary of
2293: Theorem~\ref{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}, and the four
2294: path-complete case witnesses of
2295: Proposition~\ref{prop:path-complete-case-witnesses}.  Therefore the current
2296: package instantiates the construction theorem as a certificate-construction and
2297: certificate-checking result for the finite records actually supplied, with the
2298: bounded package certificate exactness isolated in
2299: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}.
2300: \end{corollary}
2301: 
2302: \begin{proof}
2303: Under the stated support-surface condition, each cited current-package result
2304: supplies one finite hypothesis of the abstract interface.  Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger}
2305: lists the accepted rows and their evidence surfaces.  The assigned-gate boundary
2306: theorem gives the finite-record form of record-gate soundness and blocks an
2307: unrecorded implementation-soundness reading.  The machine-readable certificate
2308: records supply the schema mirror, pass-record rows, interface map, inventory,
2309: verifier script, positive verifier log, negative fixture, and negative verifier
2310: log when the exact review-bundle support surface is submitted with matching final
2311: digests.  The positive log records the invocation
2312: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py} and exit code
2313: \path{0}; the negative log records the invocation
2314: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py --negative-example review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example.json}
2315: and the expected finite rejection \path{neg1: command-run fields incomplete}.
2316: The case-witness proposition supplies
2317: the finite case-study paths used by the accepted lesson rows.  Applying
2318: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} to these records gives the stated
2319: construction; Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness} is the
2320: row-frozen exactness specialization.\end{proof}
2321: 
2322: \begin{corollary}[Finite-record boundary for external gate soundness]
2323: \label{cor:finite-record-gate-soundness-boundary}
2324: Under the hypotheses of the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, the
2325: publication-safety conclusion is invariant under replacement of the surrounding
2326: implementation by any other implementation with the same submitted acceptance
2327: entries, evidence surfaces, promotion fields, discovery-interface rows, and
2328: evidence-level boundaries.  Therefore the theorem cannot be used, without an
2329: additional submitted record-gate-soundness certificate, to infer external workflow
2330: correctness, current execution of unrecorded gates, Lean correctness, artifact
2331: semantic validity, or venue acceptance.  Conversely, any manuscript assertion
2332: that tries to promote the finite certificate constructor to one of those stronger
2333: implementation claims has a finite overclaim witness: either the required field
2334: is absent from \(\mathcal C(c)\), or the row's declared evidence level exceeds
2335: the cited certificate surface.
2336: \end{corollary}
2337: 
2338: \begin{proof}
2339: The constructor in the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, reads only the
2340: finite tuple \(\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\).  Here \(K\) is the claim-kind
2341: set assigned by the dispatch table, \(\ell\) is the declared evidence level,
2342: \(E\) is the submitted evidence surface, \(H\) is the human-promotion field,
2343: \(R\) is the finite list of recorded pass rows, \(D\) is the pinned
2344: discovery-interface field when the claim has a discovery component, and \(B\) is
2345: the evidence boundary.  If another implementation supplies the same values for
2346: these fields, the same finite schema verifier receives the same input record and
2347: therefore gives the same certificate-construction conclusion by
2348: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} and
2349: the finite-record exactness theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}.  No step of the proof inspected an
2350: unsubmitted daemon trace, reran a proof assistant, reran a finite-witness
2351: artifact, or queried a venue form after the recorded venue check; those facts are
2352: not coordinates of \(\mathcal C(c)\).
2353: 
2354: Now suppose the manuscript asserted one of the stronger implementation claims
2355: without adding a new accepted record.  External workflow correctness and current
2356: execution of unrecorded gates require pass data not present in \(R\).  Lean
2357: correctness requires a formal-source certificate rather than the bounded
2358: contextual rows allowed by Proposition~\ref{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation}.
2359: Artifact semantic validity requires a stronger artifact row than the bounded
2360: manifest records of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface}.
2361: Venue acceptance requires a venue decision rather than the submission-pack
2362: readiness row of Proposition~\ref{audit:venue-submission-pack-gate}.  In each
2363: case, either the needed finite field is missing or the row is read at a stronger
2364: evidence level than its certificate permits.  The obstruction is therefore a
2365: finite subrecord of the kind classified by
2366: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} and
2367: Theorem~\ref{thm:evidence-level-overclaim-obstruction}.\end{proof}
2368: 
2369: \begin{theorem}[Exact extension criterion for implementation-soundness readings]
2370: \label{thm:implementation-soundness-extension-criterion}
2371: Fix the dispatch table $\Gamma$ and the finite certificate schema of
2372: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}.  A reading of a
2373: load-bearing package claim as an implementation-soundness claim is accepted by
2374: the same publication-safety interface if and only if the submitted finite record
2375: surface is enlarged by an implementation-soundness extension record
2376: \[
2377: \mathsf{Impl}(c)=(K^{\mathrm{impl}},E^{\mathrm{impl}},R^{\mathrm{impl}},B^{\mathrm{impl}})
2378: \]
2379: with the following properties.
2380: \begin{enumerate}
2381: \item $K^{\mathrm{impl}}$ contains the additional implementation claim kind or
2382: claim-kind component being asserted, rather than merely reusing the original
2383: finite-record claim kind of $c$.
2384: \item $E^{\mathrm{impl}}$ is a submitted finite evidence surface naming the
2385: implementation object being certified: for example the relevant source path,
2386: version or commit, configuration, executable verifier, and implementation-log
2387: surface.
2388: \item $R^{\mathrm{impl}}$ contains a pass record, with evidence pointer inside
2389: $E^{\mathrm{impl}}$, for every gate assigned by $\Gamma$ to the enlarged claim
2390: kind $K\cup K^{\mathrm{impl}}$.
2391: \item $B^{\mathrm{impl}}$ declares the evidence level of the implementation
2392: claim and contains the command-run tuple whenever the implementation claim is
2393: asserted at command-run strength.
2394: \end{enumerate}
2395: When such an extension record is present, the finite certificate constructor of
2396: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} accepts the enlarged claim exactly
2397: at the evidence level declared by $B^{\mathrm{impl}}$.  When it is absent, every
2398: implementation-soundness reading of the same row has a finite obstruction of one
2399: of the five kinds in Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}.
2400: The current package supplies no record of the displayed form
2401: $\mathsf{Impl}(c)$ for the package verifiers or gates.  Thus the theorem is a
2402: future-facing exact criterion rather than an implementation-soundness result for
2403: this submission: it says precisely what finite record must be added before a
2404: later package may make that stronger claim.
2405: \end{theorem}
2406: 
2407: \begin{proof}
2408: First suppose the extension record is present.  Replace the original row for
2409: $c$ by the enlarged row whose claim-kind coordinate is $K\cup K^{\mathrm{impl}}$,
2410: whose evidence surface is $E\cup E^{\mathrm{impl}}$, whose pass-record field is
2411: $R\cup R^{\mathrm{impl}}$, and whose boundary field is the original boundary
2412: together with $B^{\mathrm{impl}}$.  The first condition makes the stronger
2413: reading visible to the dispatch table rather than hiding it in prose.  The second
2414: condition supplies a finite submitted surface for the implementation object.  The
2415: third condition supplies every gate record required by $\Gamma$ for the enlarged
